### Explanation of Vulnerability Demonstration
The proof-of-concept demonstrates the ReDoS vulnerability by targeting the `STRICT_SRI_REGEX` used during strict integrity parsing. The regex pattern `(\?[a-zA-Z0-9+/=]*)*$` allows optional parameters delimited by `?`. Because `?` is itself a valid character within the parameter content class `[\x21-\x7E]`, providing a string of concatenated `?` characters creates multiple ways to split the string between "start of a new group" vs "end of a previous group".

When the parser receives a terminating byte `\xA0` (which is invalid for the strict visible character class), the regex engine cannot accept the match early. Instead, it must exhaustively backtrack through all possible combinations of group boundaries to prove no valid parse exists. With 60 `?` characters, this exceeds standard safety thresholds by many orders of magnitude, resulting in the massive CPU consumption that defines the vulnerability.

